<code>assert index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()) - 1; (this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i+1)) == null) || this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i+1)));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()) - 1; (this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i + 1)) == null) || this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i + 1)));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \forall int i; index<=i && i<\old(this.size()); (\old(this.get(i)) == null && \old(this.get(i+1)) == null) || this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i+1)));</code>

This assert statement captures the condition that for every index from the given `index` to the old size minus one, either both the current and the next old elements are null, or the current element equals the next old element. This reflects the behavior of the `remove` method, where elements are shifted to the left and the last element is set to null.
